Looking at this problem, I need to determine whether the irrational numbers (with the usual topology) admit a Hausdorff compactification whose remainder is discrete.

**Answer: No.**

## Proof

Let $\mathbb{P} = \mathbb{R}\setminus\mathbb{Q}$ denote the irrationals with the usual (subspace) topology. We prove that no Hausdorff compactification of $\mathbb{P}$ has a discrete remainder.

**Key properties of $\mathbb{P}$.** The space $\mathbb{P}$ is *nowhere locally compact*: no point of $\mathbb{P}$ has a compact neighborhood. Indeed, for any $x\in\mathbb{P}$ and any $\varepsilon>0$, the set $[x-\varepsilon, x+\varepsilon]\cap\mathbb{P}$ is not compact (it is bounded but not closed in $\mathbb{R}$, since its closure in $\mathbb{R}$ is $[x-\varepsilon,x+\varepsilon]$, which contains rationals). Since every neighborhood of $x$ in $\mathbb{P}$ contains such a set, no compact neighborhood exists.

A useful consequence: **every non-empty open subset of $\mathbb{P}$ is nowhere locally compact** (hence not locally compact). To see this, let $U\subseteq\mathbb{P}$ be non-empty and open, and suppose $x\in U$ has a compact neighborhood $C$ in $U$. Then $C$ is compact in $U$, hence compact in $\mathbb{P}$ (compactness is intrinsic). Since $U$ is open in $\mathbb{P}$, the interior of $C$ in $U$ is also open in $\mathbb{P}$, so $x\in\operatorname{int}_{\mathbb{P}}(C)$, giving $x$ a compact neighborhood in $\mathbb{P}$—contradicting nowhere local compactness.

